WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.103
- [Instructor] In this video,

00:00:01.103 --> 00:00:03.210
we're gonna start thinking
about what it means

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.980
for something to be a
multiple of a number.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:05.813
So, we're asked,

00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:09.480
which of the following
numbers is a multiple of nine?

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.443
So, pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.480
All right, now let's do it together.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.410
And one way to think about a multiple,

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.680
a multiple is a number that you can get to

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.170
by multiplying the
number, in this case nine,

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.710
by a whole number.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.730
So we could figure out
the multiples of nine

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.510
by skip counting, that's one way to do it.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:31.070
So you could go from nine,

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.520
and then you add nine
to that, you get to 18.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.530
You add nine to that, you go to 27.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.710
You add nine to that, you
are going to get to 36.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.850
You add nine to that, 45.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:42.610
Add nine to that, 54.

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.950
Add nine to that, 63.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.440
Add nine to that, you get 72.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.880
Add nine to that, you get to 81.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.560
And we could keep going.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.670
But to figure out whether
these are multiples,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:54.640
you really just have to say, hey,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.240
are any of these numbers in this list?

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.730
Now, if one of these
numbers are larger than 81

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.100
we would have to keep
going to see it's included.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.500
But we can see that 46 sits
between two multiples of nine.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.090
46,

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.010
that sits closer to 45
but it sits in there,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.080
it sits between two multiples.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.200
So that's not going to be a multiple.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.680
Another way to think about it is,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:17.800
for something to be a multiple,

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.310
if you divide by nine,

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.050
you're not going to get a remainder.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.140
But if you divided 46 by nine,

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.380
you are going to get a remainder.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.750
You're not going to be able
to divide nine into it evenly.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.550
So I am just going to,

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.910
I'm going to take that
one out of the contention.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.430
77 is right over here,

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.160
it's between 72 and 81.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.690
Once again, it's between two
multiples but not a multiple.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.920
39 is between 36 and 45,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.210
so not a multiple, between two multiples.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:47.080
Rule that out.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.390
And we can see very clearly
that 18 is a multiple.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.710
If I was doing this on my own,

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.740
I would just maybe be
skip counting in my head.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.360
I'd be going, nine, all
right I don't see a nine.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.940
18, oh, I see an 18, there you go.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.170
Specially if I'm only
going to pick one choice.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:03.633
Let's do another example.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:08.560
Which of the following shows
only multiples of eight?

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.903
So, pause this video and think about that.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.040
All right, well, I could do
it choice by choice here.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:14.873
So, let's see.

00:02:14.873 --> 00:02:17.923
This first one, is four
a multiple of eight?

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.380
Well, four, it can divide into eight.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.280
We could say that eight
is a multiple of four,

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.350
but four is not a multiple of eight.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.530
What whole number am I
going to multiple eight by

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:30.840
to get to four?

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.220
So we can rule this out.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.220
And you can think about
what they're showing here.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.830
These are actually multiples of four,

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:36.720
not multiples of eight.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:37.570
We can skip count here.

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:40.870
Four, eight, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.660
These are multiples of four,
not multiples of eight.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.010
Some of the multiples of four
are also multiples of eight.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.420
Eight is a multiple of eight,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.180
16 is a multiple of eight,

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.840
24 is a multiple of eight,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.070
but not all of the multiples
of four are multiples of eight.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.980
And I think you might be
seeing a little pattern here,

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.050
which ones are multiples of eight.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.400
Now, what about this
choice right over here?

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.890
16 is eight times two.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.510
24 is eight times three.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.230
32 is eight times four.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.480
40 is eight times five.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.530
In fact, we can skip count.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:12.530
Eight then 16, 24, 32, 40, then
48, 56, so on and so forth.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.320
But these are all multiples of eight,

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.320
so I like this choice.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.200
And then over here,

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.700
one, two, four, and eight.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.060
Well, these are showing numbers

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.780
that can be divided into
eight without a remainder.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.080
You could think of them
as factors of eight.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:27.913
You could say, hey,

00:03:27.913 --> 00:03:30.070
I could multiply one
times eight to get eight.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.950
I could multiple two
times four to get eight.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.640
But these are not multiples of eight.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.160
What whole number can I
multiply eight by to get one

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.300
or to get two or to get four?

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.010
In general, your multiples of a number

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.559
are going to be that
number or larger than it.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.430
I was about to say that
number or multiples of it

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:49.263
but then I realized

00:03:49.263 --> 00:03:51.010
I can't use multiples to define multiples.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.580
It would be that number
or larger numbers than it.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.220
And if you were to skip
count with that number,

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:59.353
you would hit all of the multiples.

